// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// MS1: single-file groundwork for modules. This template edits existing docs and site pages.
// The code, the tests and the fixtures come in the tarball ms1-single-file-groundwork-replacements.tar.gz.
// Anchored edits (each anchor occurs exactly once) and two block replacements that keep their anchor lines:
//   docs/ubel.ebnf                          ImportItem with an optional `as`; the import and pub notes
//   web/site/src/reference/grammar.md       the same three edits (the page is the grammar verbatim)
//   docs/PARSER_RULES.md                    section 5.7b, two rows of the belongs-where table
//   docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md               NAME-009; NAME-002 and NAME-003 now say when they fire
//   docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md                   section 1 brought up to date, 2.5, 2.6, section 6 items 1 and 3, MS1 done
//   docs/MODULE_SYSTEM_IDEAS.md             one sentence: the gaps it names are closed
//   web/site/src/project-status.md          what landed; the three fixed known gaps out, the new one in
//   web/site/src/errors/name.md             NAME-003 gets a verified example, NAME-009 is new
//   web/site/src/errors/index.md            the NAME-003 asterisk goes, NAME-009 is listed
//   web/site/src/guide/modules.md           status note, two examples that no longer hold, the "today" and visibility sections
// This overwrites the earlier patch.mdix, which is right: there is only ever one.
// Checked on a fresh clone of live with the real applier.

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }

  ~patch_replace_block_keep<object>(_path, _start, _end, _content) {
    return {
      path         = _path
      op           = "replace_block"
      start_anchor = _start
      end_anchor   = _end
      content      = _content
      keep_anchors = "true"
    }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "(* All imports use summon. Two forms:                           *)\n(*   summon std.collections.List                                *)\n(*   from std.collections summon [List, Dictionary]            *)\n(*   from std.collections summon List                          *)\n", "(* All imports use summon. Forms:                               *)\n(*   summon std.collections.List                                *)\n(*   summon std.collections.List as L                           *)\n(*   from std.collections summon [List, Dictionary as D]       *)\n(*   from std.collections summon List as L                      *)\n(* A path under std is checked against the names the compiler   *)\n(* provides (NAME-003 when it names nothing). A path outside    *)\n(* std cannot resolve before files can be loaded (NAME-003).    *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "ImportItems   ::= \"[\" IdentList \"]\"\n                | Ident\n", "ImportItems   ::= \"[\" ImportItem (\",\" ImportItem)* \"]\"\n                | ImportItem\n\nImportItem    ::= Ident (\"as\" Ident)?\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "(* ── Constants, Statics and Type Aliases ── *)\n", "(* ── Constants, Statics and Type Aliases ── *)\n(* `pub` in front of a `const` or a `type` is recorded with the item,\n   as it is for a `static`. Nothing reads it inside one file. *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARSER_RULES.md", "### 5.8 `{` after a bare identifier — Struct Literal vs Block", "### 5.7b Imports: `as`, and what the parser does not check\n\nAn import entry is `Ident (\"as\" Ident)?`, in a `summon` and in a `from ... summon`, whether the\n`from` form takes one name or a list. `ImportItem` keeps the name and its optional rename\ntogether, so `[List as L, Dictionary]` is two entries, not three names. A missing name after `as`\nis `PARSE-001` (\"identifier after 'as'\"). The entry is kept without its rename, because dropping\nthe error would import the name un-renamed and hide the mistake.\n\nThe parser accepts any path. Whether a path names something is name resolution's job\n(`NAME-003`), and so is every rule about what a rename may be called (`NAME-002`).\n\n`pub` in front of a `const` or a `type` is parsed and stored on the item\n(`ConstDecl::visibility`, `TypeAlias::visibility`), as it is for a `static`. Nothing reads it\ninside one file.\n\n### 5.8 `{` after a bare identifier — Struct Literal vs Block")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARSER_RULES.md", "Is this `edge struct` used in the right tier? | **TierChecker**: not the parser |", "Is this `edge struct` used in the right tier? | **TierChecker**: not the parser |\n| Does this `summon` path name anything? | **Name resolution** (`NAME-003`); the parser accepts any path |\n| Do these constants and statics depend on each other in a loop? | **Name resolution** (`NAME-009`), not the parser and not the interpreter |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "| NAME-008 | StaticInConst |", "| NAME-008 | StaticInConst |\n| NAME-009 | InitializationCycle |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "already defined in this scope\". No suggestion.", "already defined in this scope\". No suggestion. A rename (`as`) onto the name of another builtin, another import or an item in the file is this error too. A builtin has no place in the source, so that report has no \"first defined here\" note.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "\"cannot resolve import path `x`\". No suggestion.", "\"cannot resolve import path `x`\". No suggestion. Reported once per path, where the `summon` is written: a member the std module does not have (`std.collections.Frobnicate`), a name from the wrong module, a module that does not exist, a module path with no member (`summon std.collections`), and any path outside `std`, which cannot resolve before files can be loaded. A `from` whose module path is bad is one report however many names follow it. The local name is still declared, so a use of it adds no `NAME-001`. The names that exist are the table in `crates/core/src/builtins/std_modules.rs`.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "A `static` initializer reading a const or another static is fine.", "A `static` initializer reading a const or another static is fine.\n- `NAME-009` `InitializationCycle` — *Error*. \"`x` depends on itself while it is being initialized\" for one item, \"initialization cycle among `a`, `b`\" for several (the constants, statics and functions on the cycle, in source order). Suggestion: break the cycle, initialize one of the items from a literal or compute it inside a function that runs after startup. A secondary span marks each other constant or static on the cycle. Fires from name resolution after every body has been walked: one node per constant, static and top-level function, an edge to every constant, static or function a body or initializer names, and a constant or static that can reach itself is on a cycle. Each separate cycle is one report. Recursion among functions alone is not a cycle. Limits: conservative, because a function that names the item on a branch that never runs during initialization is still an edge; a method called with dot syntax, a call through a function value held in a variable and a call through a trait object are not followed, and a cycle through one of those still stops the program when it starts.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_SYSTEM_IDEAS.md", "> `docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md`; this document stays as the list of parked ideas.", "> `docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md`; this document stays as the list of parked ideas. The import, rename and\n> initialization-cycle gaps named above were closed by MS1 on 2026-11-16.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-15 on live master)", "## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-15 on live master; imports, visibility and initialization updated when MS1 landed, 2026-11-16)")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "- `summon` accepts any path. `UnresolvedImport` (`NAME-003`) is defined and never reported.\n  Name resolution only registers a `DefKind::Import` with the written path and resolves nothing\n  (`crates/core/src/sema/name_resolution.rs`).\n- The interpreter ignores imports. `summon std.collections.List as L` followed by `L.new()` passes\n  `ubel check` and stops at run time with `undefined name 'L'`. `from nothing.here summon Thing`\n  does the same. Only names that are global builtins (`List`, `println`) work, and they work with\n  no `summon` at all.\n- **Bug.** `from game.util summon [clamp as c]` passes the check, and both `clamp` and `c` resolve\n  as imported names. The list loop in `parse_decl.rs` reads identifiers only, so the `as` token is\n  skipped by the progress guard and `c` is read as a second name.\n", "- `summon std.*` is checked against a table of the names the compiler provides\n  (`crates/core/src/builtins/std_modules.rs`, tested against the builtins registry). A member the\n  module does not have, a module that does not exist, a module path with no member and a path\n  outside `std` are `NAME-003`, once per path. The local name is still declared, so a use adds no\n  `undefined name`. A path outside `std` cannot resolve before multi-file loading (MS2). Name\n  resolution registers a `DefKind::Import` per name.\n- A builtin renamed by an import works: after `summon std.collections.List as L`, `L.new()` and\n  `L<int>` are the builtin `List`. Name resolution records a use of `L` as a use of the builtin,\n  type inference maps a name written in a type through `SemaContext.builtin_aliases`, and the\n  interpreter binds the new name from the program's imports. A rename may not take the name of\n  another builtin, another import or an item in the file (`NAME-002`). The interpreter ignores\n  imports in every other respect, and importing a builtin stays optional.\n- `as` inside a list (`from std.collections summon [List as L, Dictionary]`) renames one name.\n  `ImportItem` carries the name and the optional rename. A missing name after `as` is `PARSE-001`.\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "- `pub` is recorded on functions, structs, enums, traits and statics, and the AST has it on\n  fields and methods. `const` and `type` items are always declared private\n  (`pre_declare_item`), so `pub const` and `pub type` are accepted and ignored. Nothing in sema\n  or the interpreter reads visibility.\n", "- `pub` is recorded on functions, structs, enums, traits, statics, constants and type aliases\n  (the last two since MS1), and the AST has it on fields and methods. Nothing in sema or the\n  interpreter reads visibility.\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "- `const A: int = B + 1` with `const B: int = A + 1` passes `ubel check` and panics at run time\n  (\"initializing constant `A`; the constants and statics involved refer to each other\"). The same\n  holds for statics. This is the accept-then-panic class of bug.\n", "- `const A: int = B + 1` with `const B: int = A + 1` is `NAME-009`, for constants and statics\n  alike. The check also follows top-level functions called in initializers (2.5). A cycle through\n  a method, a function value or a trait object is not followed and still stops the program at\n  startup.\n- **Gap, not fixed in MS1.** A `const` initializer that calls a function which reads another\n  `const` stops at startup with `undefined name`, with no cycle involved. A function's closure is\n  a snapshot of the module scope taken before any constant is defined, and the second snapshot,\n  which includes the constants, is taken after every initializer has run. A `static` initializer\n  is not affected, because statics live in their own table. Passing the constant to the function\n  as an argument works.\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "| `from a.b summon [C, D as E]` | Imports several items, with an optional rename each (`as` inside the list is new). |", "| `from a.b summon [C, D as E]` | Imports several items, with an optional rename each (`as` inside the list landed in MS1). |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "- Constants and statics initialize once, before `main`, in dependency order across the whole\n  program. A cycle among them, through initializers, is a compile-time error that names the\n  modules and items. Today it is a run-time panic (section 1), so this closes an existing gap and\n  does not depend on multiple files.\n", "- Constants and statics initialize once, before `main`, in dependency order across the whole\n  program. A cycle among them, through initializers, is a compile-time error (`NAME-009`) that\n  names the items on the cycle. MS1 reports it within one file. The modules are named once files\n  are loaded (MS2).\n- The check builds a graph with one node per constant, static and top-level function, and an edge\n  to every constant, static or function a body or initializer names. A constant or static that\n  can reach itself is on a cycle. Each separate cycle is one report. Recursion among functions\n  alone is not a cycle.\n- Limits, stated so the check is not read as exact. It is conservative: a function that names the\n  constant on a branch that never runs during initialization is still an edge, so\n  `const A = f(false)` with `fn f(b: bool) int { if b { return A } return 0 }` is rejected. It\n  does not follow a method called with dot syntax, a call through a function value held in a\n  variable, or a call through a trait object, and a cycle through one of those still stops the\n  program when it starts. A closure written inside a function body counts as part of that\n  function.\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "- `summon std.*` is checked against a table of the names the compiler provides. An unknown path is\n  `NAME-003`, which is finally reported. The checked form stays optional for builtins.\n", "- `summon std.*` is checked against a table of the names the compiler provides, grouped as\n  `std.collections` (`List`, `Dictionary`, `Set`, `Queue`, `Stack`, `InlineList`), `std.memory`\n  (`Pool`, `Unique`, `Shared`, `SyncShared`), `std.io` (`println`, `print`, `log`), `std.debug`\n  (`assert`, `panic`, `typeof`), `std.math` (`Math`, `sqrt`, `abs`, `min`, `max`, `floor`, `ceil`,\n  `range`) and `std.convert` (`to_string`, `to_int`, `to_float`, `to_double`). An unknown path is\n  `NAME-003`. The checked form stays optional for builtins. A test fails when a builtin is added\n  to the registry and not placed in the table.\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "3. **The table behind `summon std.*`.** Which names and which grouping (`std.collections`,\n   `std.io`, `std.fmt`, and the rest), taken from the builtins registry.\n", "3. **The table behind `summon std.*`.** Built in MS1 from the builtins registry (2.6). The grouping\n   is a first cut, not a decision: only `std.collections.List` and `std.io.println` were fixed by\n   an existing fixture. A name can move between modules while no program depends on it.\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "   import may not, because a builtin never needs summoning.", "   import may not, because a builtin never needs summoning. MS1 enforces the import half: a\n   rename may not take the name of another builtin (`NAME-002`).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "| **MS1** | Single-file groundwork that fixes bugs which exist today: `as` inside an import list, `summon std.*` validated (`NAME-003` reported, so an alias of a builtin works or fails at check time), a compile-time initialization-cycle error for constants and statics, `pub const` and `pub type` recorded, attributes on the package line. | MS0 |", "| **MS1** | **Done 2026-11-16.** Single-file groundwork that fixed bugs which existed: `as` inside an import list, `summon std.*` validated (`NAME-003`) and a builtin rename that works, a compile-time initialization-cycle error for constants and statics (`NAME-009`), `pub const` and `pub type` recorded. Attributes on the package line moved to MS5, because parsing `@tier(low) package a.b` without applying it would add a new accept-and-ignore gap. | MS0 |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MODULE_DESIGN.md", "| **MS5** | The module default tier (M5) and `@cfg` on the package line. | MS1 |", "| **MS5** | The module default tier (M5) and attributes on the package line (`@tier`, `@cfg`). | MS1 |")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "  Next: single-file fixes for imports and initialization cycles, then multi-file checking", "  Single-file groundwork has landed: `summon std...` paths are checked, `as` renames a builtin,\n  and a constant or static that depends on itself is an error. Next: multi-file checking")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "  `@tier(...)` written on a `const` is now an error rather than ignored", "  `@tier(...)` written on a `const` is now an error rather than ignored\n- Imports are checked: `summon std.collections.List` and `from std.io summon [println as say]`\n  name something the compiler provides or are `NAME-003`, and `as` renames a builtin (`L.new()`,\n  `L<int>`, `say(...)`). `pub const` and `pub type` are accepted and recorded\n- A `const` or `static` that depends on itself, directly or through the functions its initializer\n  calls, is `NAME-009` instead of a failure at startup")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "- `summon` accepts any path and the interpreter ignores imports, so\n  `summon std.collections.List as L` followed by `L.new()` passes the check and stops with\n  `undefined name 'L'` when the program runs\n- `from m summon [a as b]` is accepted and imports both `a` and `b` as separate names\n- Constants or statics that depend on each other circularly pass the check and stop with\n  `undefined name` when the program runs\n", "- A `const` initializer that calls a function which reads another `const` stops with\n  `undefined name` when the program runs, with no cycle involved. Pass the constant to the\n  function as an argument. A cycle through a method, a function value or a trait object passes\n  the check and stops the program at startup\n- The interpreter ignores imports except to rename a builtin, and a path outside `std` is an\n  error until several files can be loaded\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/name.md", "A `summon` path could not be resolved.\n\n*No verified example yet.*\n\n**Fix:** Check the path.\n", "A `summon` path names nothing the compiler provides. Under `std` that is a member the module does not have, a module that does not exist, or a module path with no member. A path outside `std` cannot resolve yet, because modules are not loaded from other files. A `from` with a bad module path is one report however many names follow it, and the names are still declared, so their uses add no `undefined name`.\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error NAME-003 -->\n```ubel\nsummon std.collections.Frobnicate\n\nfn main() void {\n    println(\"hello\")\n}\n```\n\n```text\n>>> error[NAME-003]: cannot resolve import path `std.collections.Frobnicate`\n  --> 1:1\n    |\n  1 | summon std.collections.Frobnicate\n    | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n<<<\n```\n\n**Fix:** Check the path against the names the compiler provides: `std.collections` (`List`, `Dictionary`, `Set`, `Queue`, `Stack`, `InlineList`), `std.memory`, `std.io`, `std.debug`, `std.math` and `std.convert`. Importing a builtin is optional, since every one is available without a `summon`.\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/name.md", "**Fix:** Make the other item a `const` too, or initialise this value from a `static`.", "**Fix:** Make the other item a `const` too, or initialise this value from a `static`.\n\n### NAME-009\n\n**InitializationCycle** — `` initialization cycle among `a`, `b` `` (or `` `x` depends on itself while it is being initialized `` for one item)\n\nA `const` or `static` is evaluated once, before `main`, so one that depends on itself, directly or through other initializers, has no value to start from. The check also follows the top-level functions an initializer calls. Each separate cycle is one report, naming the items on it. It is conservative about a function that names the item on a branch that never runs, and it does not follow a method called with dot syntax, a function value or a trait object. A cycle through one of those stops the program when it starts.\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error NAME-009 -->\n```ubel\nconst A: int = B + 1\nconst B: int = A + 1\n\nfn main() void {\n    println($\"{A}\")\n}\n```\n\n```text\n>>> error[NAME-009]: initialization cycle among `A`, `B`\n  --> 1:1\n    |\n  1 | const A: int = B + 1\n    | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  ~> note: `B` is on the cycle\n    --> 2:1\n      |\n    2 | const B: int = A + 1\n      | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  <~\n  ~> help: break the cycle: initialize one of the items from a literal, or compute it inside a function that runs after startup\n  <~\n<<<\n```\n\n**Fix:** Break the cycle: initialise one of the items from a literal, or compute it inside a function that runs after startup.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/index.md", "| [NAME-003](./name.md#name-003) | UnresolvedImport \\* |", "| [NAME-003](./name.md#name-003) | UnresolvedImport |")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/index.md", "| [NAME-008](./name.md#name-008) | StaticInConst |", "| [NAME-008](./name.md#name-008) | StaticInConst |\n| [NAME-009](./name.md#name-009) | InitializationCycle |")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "(* All imports use summon. Two forms:                           *)\n(*   summon std.collections.List                                *)\n(*   from std.collections summon [List, Dictionary]            *)\n(*   from std.collections summon List                          *)\n", "(* All imports use summon. Forms:                               *)\n(*   summon std.collections.List                                *)\n(*   summon std.collections.List as L                           *)\n(*   from std.collections summon [List, Dictionary as D]       *)\n(*   from std.collections summon List as L                      *)\n(* A path under std is checked against the names the compiler   *)\n(* provides (NAME-003 when it names nothing). A path outside    *)\n(* std cannot resolve before files can be loaded (NAME-003).    *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "ImportItems   ::= \"[\" IdentList \"]\"\n                | Ident\n", "ImportItems   ::= \"[\" ImportItem (\",\" ImportItem)* \"]\"\n                | ImportItem\n\nImportItem    ::= Ident (\"as\" Ident)?\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "(* ── Constants, Statics and Type Aliases ── *)\n", "(* ── Constants, Statics and Type Aliases ── *)\n(* `pub` in front of a `const` or a `type` is recorded with the item,\n   as it is for a `static`. Nothing reads it inside one file. *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "> **Status: syntax only.** Import statements are parsed, but nothing is loaded\n> or resolved: there is no module resolution, no standard library to import\n> from, and no multi-file builds. The design was decided on 2026-11-15 and is\n> summarised at the end; none of it is built. This page describes what you can\n> write today and what that actually does.", "> **Status: single-file imports.** A `summon std...` path is checked against the\n> names the compiler provides, and `as` renames a built-in. Nothing is loaded\n> from another file: there is no module resolution beyond `std` and no\n> multi-file builds. The design was decided on 2026-11-15 and is summarised at\n> the end; the multi-file part is not built. This page describes what you can\n> write today and what that actually does.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "from std.core summon Option                // or a single one", "from std.math summon sqrt                  // or a single one")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "    println(\"imports parse\")", "    println(\"imports resolve\")")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "```text\nimports parse\n```", "```text\nimports resolve\n```")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "| `from a.b summon [C, D]` | several names from one place |", "| `from a.b summon [C, D as E]` | several names from one place, each optionally renamed |")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "re-exports. Single-file fixes (imports that are checked, initialisation\ncycles) come before multi-file checking. Until then, everything above under\n\"What happens today\" is the behaviour.", "re-exports. The single-file fixes (imports that are checked, a rename of a\nbuilt-in, initialisation cycles) have landed. Multi-file checking is next. Until\nit lands, everything above under \"What happens today\" is the behaviour.")
    patch_replace_block_keep("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "## What happens today", "- **Imports go at the top of the file.** A `summon` inside a function, or between", "\nThe names the language provides, such as `println`, `List`, `Dictionary` and\n`range`, are available **without** any import (see\n[Built-in functions](../reference/builtins.md)), so an import is optional. This\nis what an import does today:\n\n- **A path under `std` is checked.** `summon std.collections.List` and\n  `from std.io summon [print, println]` name something the compiler provides. A\n  member the module does not have, a module that does not exist, a module path\n  with no member, and any path outside `std` are\n  [NAME-003](../errors/name.md#name-003). A path outside `std` cannot resolve\n  yet, because nothing is loaded from another file:\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error NAME-003 -->\n```ubel\nsummon nothing.here.Thing\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\n- **`as` renames a built-in.** The new name works wherever the built-in does: in\n  a type, in a constructor call and as a function. The original name keeps\n  working beside it:\n\n<!-- ubel-test: run -->\n```ubel\nsummon std.collections.List as L\nfrom std.io summon [println as say]\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut xs: L<int> = L.new()\n    xs.push(3)\n    say(xs.len())\n}\n```\n\n```text\n1\n```\n\n  A new name may not be the name of another built-in, of another import or of an\n  item in the file ([NAME-002](../errors/name.md#name-002)).\n\n- **`as` works inside a `from` list.**\n  `from std.collections summon [List as L, Dictionary]` renames `List` and\n  leaves `Dictionary` alone. A missing name after `as` is a parse error\n  ([PARSE-001](../errors/parse.md#parse-001)).\n")
    patch_replace_block_keep("web/site/src/guide/modules.md", "## Visibility", "## The decided design (not built)", "\n`pub` marks an item public. It is recorded on functions, structs, enums, traits,\nstatics, constants and type aliases, but nothing reads it yet: today everything\nin a file is visible to everything else in it.\n\n")
)
